 up 18%):** Its C$2.4B equity offering was pulled on 14 October after demand fell short. Caldria Ratings then put it on negative watch, which makes more debt costlier and harder to raise. Pellham has said it will defer two substation programmes until financing improves. It will also cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and resize its Q1 tender, which was about C$900M. Pellham's own actions show it can't finance the full plan.\n- **Spreads:** Utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 2026 to 165bp, from 130bp in Q1 and 135bp in Q2. Debt is still open, as Norvane's deal shows, but it is more expensive.\n- **Not found:** I found no data on policy rates, total issuance volumes, or leverage and debt capacity (maturity profiles, covenant headroom). I'm inferring capacity from Pellham's actions and its rating watch, not from balance-sheet figures.\n\nDebt markets are open but costlier, and the shortfall is at Pellham, which is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria. For VLTX, expect the Pellham tender to shrink or slip, even though Norvane's looks funded. VLTX holdings won 60% of the last tender round.\n\nSources: the Caldrian utility credit conditions note for Q3 2026, the equity issuance tracker, Pellham's market statement, the Caldria Ratings watch list, the 2027 tender calendar, and the `utility_spreads` series.","ttft_ms":3413,"type":"result","duration_ms":53033,"uuid":"564d81f7-4b3f-4f39-8be8-b2edc0114dd0","ttft_stream_ms":2517,"time_to_request_ms":58,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":4,"system_prompt":3,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":13,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":6,"init_frame_send":1,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":6,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":2,"request_build":4},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","